ow a top gun pilot  rooster's a chip off the old block  there's that mustache and hair the  hawaiian shirt and he sings great balls  of fire at the piano which apparently  miles teller learnt for the movie and  plays for real obviously given their  history maverick's relationship with  rooster is more complicated than it was  with goose expect lots of drama  including a heady mix of resentment  guilt and a barrel full of tension  between them plus his father's fate  means both that rooster can be a bit  more cautious flying compared to other  pilots and that maverick wants to look  out for him like a father figure  maverick's old nemesis iceman is now an  admiral and is crucial to the plot as he  insists on mav being posted back to top  gun to train its graduates for a complex  special mission you were here at the  request of iceman he seems to think that  you have something left to offer the  navy actor val kilmer returns in a short  but touching cameo which sees the  friends and former rivals discuss the  dangerous new operation and their shared  past much of iceman's side of the  conversation is tapped out on a computer  as kilmer's real-life experience with  throat cancer is woven into his  character's story the whole scene is  particularly poignant especially for  fans who grew up watching the original  film you can be my wingman anytime you  can be mine as for the spirit of the  young 1980s iceman that lives on in  hangman a cocky new top gun graduate  played by glenn powell what do we have  here yeah here i thought we were special  fellas this here's bagman hangman whose  hostility towards rooster has definite  shades of iceman and maverick back in  the day with a neat twist on the 80s  plot while val kilmer returns in person  and a young anthony edwards and his  on-screen wife meg ryan return in  flashbacks the original film's female  lead astrophysicist and top gun civilian  contractor charlie played by kelly  mcgillis isn't even name-dropped in  passing despite her a maverick ending up  together instead cruz's on-screen love  interest this time is penny benjamin  owner of aviators club the hard deck who  was briefly mentioned in the first movie  and one admiral's daughter penny  benjamin and is now brought to life by  jennifer connolly while there was no  previous history between charlie and  maverick when they kicked off their  romance back in the 80s penny and  maverick's relationship has been on and  off many times over the years but maybe  now it's time to fly off into the sunset  both military and movie technologies  come a long way since the mid-80s and  that's clearly reflected in the sequel  while for other movies that might mean  stacks of cgi sequences this is a tom  cruise production an actor who's not  just known for his star power but also  for his perfectionism and punch on for  old school movie making so the emphasis  is very much on doing as many practical  stunts as possible in fact cruz who's  famous for doing lots of his own action  on land and in the air reportedly also  took the time to create bespoke aviation  programs for the movie's young cast who  did several months of intense flight  training that meant that while the  planes were flown by real navy pilots  the filmmakers were able to shoot the  young actors in the air really reacting  to the need for speed in other words the  kind of stomach churning g-force they'd  feel in real life the result is that  often it feels as if you're in the air  with them soaring and diving through the  skies during the movie's plentiful  scenes of fast-paced aerobatics and  dogfights which are exactly the kind of  thing made to be seen on the most  massive screen available as well as the  newer planes appearing in the sequel one  of the main fighter aircrafts from the  80s movie the f-14 tomcat is back in  action and also just like in the 80s the  enemy remains nameless understandably  given the first film was introducing  audiences to the world of the navy's